[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted: browser + [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  [1mNext fork:[0m what actually goes in there. The app has no settings today, so the first set defines the schema — and one
  candidate carries a security consequence worth naming up front.

  - [1mInfrastructure + one demo setting (e.g. theme)[0m — builds the storage module, defaults, and a settings surface with a
    single harmless value. Smallest schema to get right, easy to add settings later.
  - [1mTheme + "remember username"[0m — the obvious thing to persist on a login page, but it writes a user identifier to
    [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, readable by any script on the origin (including via XSS) and left on shared machines. To be explicit:
    I'd persist the [3musername only[0m — never the password, and never a session token, both of which belong nowhere near
    this store.
  - [1mA specific list you have in mind[0m — tell me and I'll design to it.

  Adding settings later is cheap. The storage key naming, the versioning of the stored blob, and whether identifiers are
  allowed in it are the parts that get expensive to change once data exists in real browsers.

  [1mMy recommendation: infrastructure + theme only.[0m It gets the persistence layer right without putting a user identifier
  into browser storage on day one, and "remember username" can be added deliberately later as its own decision.
